components: a bare git repository with a post-receive hook, and a web server. Here's the complete setup:\n\n## Server Setup\n\nRun these commands on your server as the user you'll be cloning as:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Create the git repository directory\nmkdir -p /git/server\ncd /git/server\ngit init --bare\n\n# 2. Create the web root directory\nmkdir -p /var/www/html\n\n# 3. Create the post-receive hook\ncat > /git/server/hooks/post-receive << 'EOF'\n#!/bin/bash\nDEPLOY_DIR=\"/var/www/html\"\ngit --work-tree=$DEPLOY_DIR --git-dir=/git/server checkout -f\nEOF\n\n# 4. Make the hook executable\nchmod +x /git/server/hooks/post-receive\n```\n\n## Web Server Setup\n\nInstall and start a simple HTTP server (choose one):\n\n**Option 1: Python (simplest)**\n```bash\n# Run in /var/www/html directory\ncd /var/www/html\npython3 -m http.server 8080\n```\n\n**Option 2: Node.js**\n```bash\ncd /var/www/html\nnpx http-server -p 8080\n```\n\n**Option 3: nginx (production)**\n```bash\nsudo apt-get install nginx\nsudo tee /etc/nginx/sites-available/default > /dev/null <<'EOF'\nserver {\n    listen 8080;\n    server_name _;\n    root /var/www/html;\n    index index.html;\n    location / {\n        try_files $uri $uri/ =404;\n    }\n}\nEOF\nsudo systemctl restart nginx\n```\n\n## How It Works\n\n1. When you push to the git server, the `post-receive` hook automatically checks out the files to `/var/www/html`\n2. The web server serves those files on port 8080\n3. Your workflow becomes:\n   ```bash\n   git clone user@server:/git/server\n   cd server\n   echo \"hello world\" > hello.html\n   git add hello.html\n   git commit -m \"add hello.html\"\n   git push origin master\n   ```\n4. After the push completes, curl will show the file:\n   ```bash\n   curl http://server:8080/hello.html\n   # Output: hello world\n   ```\n\n## Notes\n\n- The post-receive hook runs with the permissions of the git user, so make sure `/var/www/html` is writable by that user (adjust permissions if needed: `chmod -R 755 /var/www/html`)\n- The bare git repository is in `/git/server` and the actual web files are checked out to `/var/www/html`\n- You can scale this by adding more sophisticated deployment logic in the post-receive hook (run build commands, restart services, etc.)\n\nLet me know if you need help with any part of this setup!","ttft_ms":4375,"type":"result","duration_ms":11095,"uuid":"3646ebf6-7a10-49df-9461-bc3c8aab3ca4","ttft_stream_ms":880,"time_to_request_ms":43,"queued_turn_count":0}
